Author: frank <hellmann@pik-potsdam.de>
Date:   Sun Nov 22 23:13:05 2026 +0100

    Compiler: an answer about a set that inference does not fold is not consistent; a set given as a constant is not evaluated at compile time
    
    Concrete evaluation runs a call at compile time where its effects are consistent, and folds the result with an
    edge to the callee's code only (compiler §3.1). A test about a set that inference left unfolded (`isa`, `<:`, a
    `typeassert`, `new`'s field test) answers by the world's memberships, and its code records nothing for it; and
    `typeintersect` and `==` of types assert their consistency (`@_total_meta`) over the runtime's answer. So a
    caller folded `Tuple{S} <: Tuple{X}`, `typeintersect(S, X) === Union{}`, `A == B` or a typeassert's throw in the
    world it was compiled in, and kept it after a membership (E-542). Now such a builtin's call and such a field
    test are not consistent, and a call given as a constant a type in which an open set occurs is not consistent and
    is not evaluated at compile time (`names_open_set`, `names_open_set_const`, `concrete_eval_eligible`).
    
    Reference: compiler §4.8 (code that relies on an answer about a set holds only where it does).
    Test: experiments/implementation/audit/set_folds.jl (E-542); experiments/implementation/w171/gates.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

